WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.510
all right, let's dive into the economic policy
known as a value added tax or a back or a

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.510
bat tax.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.950
Although bat tax doesn't really make sense
because it makes it a value added tax tax,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.900
but sometimes people say that tax,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.529
this has been coming up a lot more lately,
particularly because democratic presidential

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:21.440
candidate Andrew Yang has been talking about
that taxes, more people have gotten interested

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.570
in the policy and essentially a that is a
tax on consumption.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:32.559
It's on stuff that you buy and it's implemented
at different phases in the production of the

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:35.480
good where value is added.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.780
There's a lot of different countries that
have that.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.179
Taxes have different kinds.

00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:44.219
If you look at the countries that have that
taxes, there's not really a trend about where

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:48.059
we say, well countries with VAT taxes tend
to be higher income countries or lower income

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:50.340
countries or countries with that.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.780
Taxes tend to have higher deficits or lowered
efforts.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:59.549
It's just really all over the map, figuratively
and literally and people who like that taxes

00:00:59.549 --> 00:01:05.630
often say one of the pros is you generate
income for the government to then use for

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:11.229
stuff without just going after the wealthy
with like a higher top marginal tax rate,

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:12.400
for example.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.340
But that also relates to the primary critique
of a VAT tax, which is that it's regressive,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:24.090
meaning it is more attacks on the income of
poor people than rich people as a percentage

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.329
of their total income.

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:29.799
So imagine that you put a 5% VAT on computers.

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:35.670
Rich people might consume more computers than
poor people, but as a percentage of their

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.560
income poor will pay more to a VAT tax than
rich people.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.369
If you put a 5% VAT tax on computers.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:51.700
Now another pro of the VAT tax is that it
is claimed to be harder to avoid a VAT tax

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.319
with some of the tax loopholes that let you
avoid other types of taxes.

00:01:56.319 --> 00:02:01.979
And the reason for that is that the, that
tax is added at each stage where value is

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:07.549
added to a product instead of quote at the
register like a sales tax.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:14.420
And so it doesn't disincentivize income like
a high top marginal income tax rate might.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.769
And we're going to deal with that later as
well.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:24.379
Now Andrew Yang's proposal includes the idea
of exempting some staples and putting sort

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:30.129
of like a luxury VAT tax on luxury items that
only rich people are likely to consume or

00:02:30.129 --> 00:02:31.129
purchase.

00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:35.690
And that that in principle would deal with
the regressive nature of the VAT tax.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:42.720
So yang says staples would have low or no
VAT tax and basic goods would have low or

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.739
no VAT tax, but then luxury goods would have
a higher VAT tax.

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:49.720
That's a perfectly reasonable idea.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.690
But there's very good research from the tax
policy center about this, which says you can

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:59.760
reduce the degree to which of that tax is
regressive by exempting some staples and doing

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.000
higher VAT taxes on luxury goods, but that
the high income households consume, um, uh,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.689
more of those, uh, luxury goods, but it's
still less as a share of income and it still

00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:17.970
ends up being a regressive tax in total, just
less so.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.390
Now there are some inaccuracies in some of
the things that Andrew Yang has said about

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:23.390
the VAT tax.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:28.030
He will often flatly say the VAT tax already
works in a hundred countries.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:35.660
You know, it, that taxes exist in many countries,
different parameters in different countries.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.739
There's different ways of measuring what success
would be.

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:44.650
The blanket statement that VAT taxes succeed
or are working well in a hundred countries.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.440
It's not a very meaningful statement to make.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.290
Now there's an alternative to a VAT tax, which
is a refundable tax credit for a fixed number

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.650
of dollars that could be proposed.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.750
And with that, everybody gets the same amount.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.510
If you earn less.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:06.359
It's progressive in the sense that you get
a higher portion of your income refunded if

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:12.219
you earn less, and it's the exact opposite
of a VAT in terms of this is a progressive

00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:15.989
tool, whereas the VAT tax is a regressive
tool.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:19.780
So in total, what do we know about that taxes
overall?

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.830
Number one, they are regressive.

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:22.830
Okay.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:27.310
That taxes hit lower income people more than
higher income people even with a higher tax

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:30.170
of that tax on luxury goods even exempting
some staples.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:33.120
That's what the tax policy centers research
tells us.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.800
Number two, that taxes do tend to decrease
consumer spending overall.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.120
And number three, that taxes do tend to increase
the cost of goods at their final retail or

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.470
consumer price.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:50.380
Even though VAT taxes are levied at different
stages in the production process where value

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:55.780
is added a lot or all of that is ultimately
tacked on at the end to the retail price of

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:56.780
a product.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.390
So does that mean of that tax is bad?

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:04.380
No, I mean it just like any policy that taxes
do raise money for governments and then that

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.960
is money that government can use to pay for
things.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.100
There are many policies that have similar
consequences where they either increase prices

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.670
or they are regressive.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:19.270
That doesn't mean it's a policy that should
never be considered or that it's a policy

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.630
that should definitely be considered with
any policy.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.690
We have to weigh the pros and cons.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:29.100
We have to consider whether there are better
tools available that might more directly get

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.780
us to our goal.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.420
We have to consider what is considered fair.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.310
When we think about who should be taxed.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.640
Then at what levels, and then we have to also
think about what behaviors are we trying to

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:48.550
incentivize or disincentivize what economic
activity would have that tax and courage or

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:53.760
disencourage unencouraged this incentivize
and then we figure out whether a VAT tax makes

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:54.760
sense.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.130
There's no universal right or wrong with these
things.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.550
There are different tools for different scenarios.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:00.550
That tax is one of those.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.550
There is a story that's hugely important but
not necessarily for the reasons a lot of people

